{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>If there arises … for judgment.</b> It was necessary to say ״לַמִּשְׁפָּט״ (for judgment), to signal that these laws were stated only regarding a sage who is qualified to instruct in matters of judgment, and so they said in the Mishnah (Sanhedrin 86a): a student who is not qualified to issue a ruling is exempt. However, I saw in the Baraita (Sanhedrin 87a) that they derived the exemption of a student who is not qualified to issue a ruling from the phrase ״כִּי יִפָּלֵא״ — [meaning] one who is מֻפְלָא (outstanding) in the court. And Rashi and Tosafot, in [the chapter] הנחנקין, explained that מֻפְלָא means an expert, and [serves] to exclude a student. And the Rambam explained (Hilkhot Mamrim 3:5) that the intent is: one from whom nothing is נִפְלֶה (extraordinary) except an extraordinary matter. And accordingly, according to this [interpretation], the verse says <b>לַמִּשְׁפָּט</b> to say that the law stated regarding this elder applies only to a ruling for practical action, as they said in the Mishnah there, and this is their language: he is not liable until he instructs [them] to act, as it is said ״וְהָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה בְזָדוֹן״, end quote. Now, from this verse there is proof only regarding one who acted upon his ruling, not if he instructed others to act, and they [the Sages] derive [the latter obligation] from the phrase ״לַמִּשְׁפָּט״ — that if he instructed others, he is liable." "<b>Or</b> it may intend this way: <b>If there arises</b> etc. <b>for judgment</b> — even if it was a practical halakhah and he instructed [them] to act contrary to the intent of the disputants, and the law turns out not to be so; even though he instructed in a matter whose intentional violation incurs karet, etc., he is not put to death. That is, even though he instructed [them] to act contrary to the majority who dispute him, he is not put to death until he instructs [them] to act contrary to what the Great Court in the Chamber of Hewn Stone will declare concerning it. And so the matter is proven in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 88b)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:55.988247058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}